PyThaiNLP 5.4 corpus data (https://github.com/PyThaiNLP/pythainlp; code Apache-2.0, word lists
CC0-1.0, Copyright the PyThaiNLP project): words_th.txt and tnc_freq.txt (Thai National Corpus
frequencies) are the source of the Thai word list src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/th/words.txt
(generated by scripts/frontend/build_th_lexicon.py; PyThaiNLP itself is a development dependency
only). TLTK (Thai Language Toolkit, BSD-3-Clause) was used only to measure agreement; nothing
derived from it is included.

German front-end: the stem list, exception tables and word lists in src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/de/
are hand-written for this project (Apache-2.0); German Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA) was used only to
find frequent words to check, no text is included. eSpeak NG (GPL-3.0) was used only to measure
agreement (scripts/frontend/eval_de_g2p.py); nothing derived from it is included.
eSpeak NG (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng, GPL-3.0) is called as an external program by
scripts/frontend/eval_latin_g2p.py only to measure the agreement of the Italian and Turkish
front-ends; it is not a dependency and nothing derived from it is included. The Italian and
Turkish tables (src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/it/, lexicons/tr/) are hand-written.
Koziev/NLP_Datasets (https://github.com/Koziev/NLP_Datasets, CC0-1.0, Ilya Koziev): the stressed
word forms of Stress/all_accents.tsv are the source of the Russian stress tables
src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/ru/stress_suffixes.tsv and stress.tsv (generated by
scripts/frontend/build_ru_stress.py). Russian Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA) was used only to count
words (which forms to keep, which words are written with ё); no text is included. eSpeak NG
(GPL-3.0) was used only to measure agreement (scripts/frontend/eval_ru_g2p.py, eval_pl_g2p.py);
nothing derived from it is included. Polish Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA) was used only as synthesis
input and to look for pronunciation exceptions; no text is included.
QCRI WikiNews diacritized test set (github.com/kdarwish/Farasa, WikiNewsTruth.txt; "feel free
to copy and distribute"; Qatar Computing Research Institute) was used only to measure the Arabic
vowel restoration (scripts/frontend/eval_ar_vowels.py); nothing from it is included. The Arabic
word table src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/ar/words.tsv is hand-written from the maintainer's and
Claude's own knowledge of Arabic; no entry is copied or derived from Tashkeela, SAMA/Buckwalter,
Mishkal/Arramooz, CAMeL or any other GPL, LDC or CC BY-SA resource. Arabic Wikipedia text (CC
BY-SA) was used only to choose which words are frequent.
eSpeak NG (GPL-3.0, https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng) was used only to measure the agreement
of the Finnish and Greek front-ends (scripts/frontend/eval_espeak_fi_el.py); nothing derived from it
is included. Finnish and Greek Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA) is used only as synthesis input and is
not included.
Malay: the tables in src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/ms/ are hand-written for this project. eSpeak NG
(GPL-3.0, run as an external program) was used only to measure agreement; nothing derived from it
is included.

Tagalog: the tables in src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/tl/ are hand-written for this project.
Wiktionary pronunciations (CC BY-SA, through a kaikki.org extract) were used only to measure
agreement at evaluation time; nothing derived from them is included.

Swahili: the tables in src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/sw/ are hand-written for this project. eSpeak
NG (GPL-3.0, run as an external program) was used only to measure agreement; nothing derived from
it is included.
eSpeak NG (GPL-3.0) was used only to measure the agreement of the Dutch, Swedish and Norwegian
front-ends (scripts/frontend/eval_g2p_espeak.py); nothing derived from it is included. Their rules,
exception tables and word lists (src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/nl, sv, no) are hand-written.

Google language-resources (https://github.com/google/language-resources), Khmer pronunciation
lexicon km/data/lexicon.tsv (CC BY 4.0, Copyright 2018 Google Inc.): the Khmer word list, onset and
rhyme tables and override readings in src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/km/ are derived from it
(scripts/frontend/build_km_lexicon.py). Khmer Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA) was used only to count how
often each lexicon word occurs; no text is included.

Google language-resources, Burmese pronunciation grammar my/burmese.grm and Zawgyi test
pairs my/zawgyi_unicode_test.tsv (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2015, 2016 Google Inc.): the onset, rhyme and
syllable-exception rules of src/vtts/frontend/my/g2p_my.py follow the grammar; the test pairs were
used to check the Zawgyi converter. English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA, via kaikki.org) was used only to
measure agreement (scripts/frontend/eval_my_g2p.py); nothing derived from it is included.

Development tools: much of the code, the training and data pipelines, the firmware and the
documentation were written with Claude Code (Anthropic, https://claude.com/claude-code), directed
and reviewed by the maintainer. Commits made this way carry a "Co-Authored-By: Claude" trailer.

NST pronunciation lexicon for Danish (Nordic Language Technology; distributed by the National
Library of Norway, Språkbanken, https://www.nb.no/sprakbanken/, under CC0 1.0): the source of the
Danish readings in src/vtts/frontend/lexicons/da/lexicon.tsv and words.txt (generated by
scripts/frontend/build_da_lexicon.py). Danish Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA) was used only to choose
